{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Baumeister1969-TableIII-Pd-absorber",
  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Radiant power flow and absorptance in thin films,\" Appl. Opt. 8, 423-436 (1969). doi:10.1364/AO.8.000423 - Table III, p. 427 (Sec. IV)",
  "Notes": "Absorbing coating maximising the absorptance of a 200 A palladium film at 5461 A with five dielectric layers (indices constrained to 1.38-2.32)\nSpecification: Maximise absorptance of the Pd film at lambda0 = 5461 A using five dielectric layers with 1.38 <= n <= 2.32.\nReported: p. 427: maximum absorptance 94.20 % at 5461 A. Four dielectric layers (3-6) form the matching stack, layer 1 is a single-layer antireflection coating. Check computed here: A = 0.9420, R = 0.0000, T = 0.0580 at 546.1 nm.\nNotes: Table III lists from incident medium (air, n = 1.00) to substrate (n = 1.52), same order as here. Footnote: dielectric thicknesses are quarter-wave optical thickness - the 'Thickness in mu' column is the wavelength at which each layer is a quarter wave, so 0.5461 = 1 QWOT at 546.1 nm, L'' = 0.4096/0.5461 QWOT, L' = 0.4915/0.5461 QWOT; Pd is 200 A physical (0.0200 um). Layer labels: 1 L'' (n = 1.87), 2 Pd, 3 L', 4 H, 5 L, 6 H. Pd index printed as 2.3 - j2.70 (k = +2.70 here). Absorptance of the whole stack equals the Pd absorptance (dielectrics lossless). Single-wavelength design; band_nm is the design wavelength only. 6 layers, matches the table.\nLayers from Table III, p. 427 (Sec. IV); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours A 94.20 % at 546.1 nm; paper A 94.20 %.",
